{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087693 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087500 :question/math-academy-id "e-4367" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix ${A},$ where \n\n$$\n{A}=\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix ${A}$ has $4$ rows and $3$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $4\\times 3.$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087982 :question/math-academy-id "q-11454" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix $A$, where\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 8 & -3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$?" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ has $2$ rows and $4$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $2 \\times 4$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088348 :question/math-academy-id "q-7806" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix $C$, where\n$C = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 4 & 6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$?" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $C$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $2 \\times 3$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235398 :question/math-academy-id "q-139406" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix $P$, where\n\n$$\nP = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe dimensions of $P$ are {{field-1}} $\\times$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $P$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259822 :question/math-academy-id "q-125527" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix $S$, where $S=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & -5 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1 & -4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -3 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 11 & 0 & 0 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $S$ has $6$ rows and $6$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $6\\times 6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259823 :question/math-academy-id "q-125528" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix $T$, where $T=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & -23 \\\\ 4 & -\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $T$ has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $2\\times 2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259824 :question/math-academy-id "q-125529" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix $Y$, where $Y=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{7} & 10 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & -\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $Y$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299741 :question/math-academy-id "q-319310" :question/problem "Let $K=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 0 & -\\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & \\frac{1}{3} & -4 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 & 6 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nThe dimensions of the matrix $K$ are {{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $K$ has $5$ rows and $5$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $5\\times 5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335982 :question/math-academy-id "q-69542" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix $K$, where\n$K=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $K$ has $4$ rows and $2$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $4\\times 2.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087694 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087501 :question/math-academy-id "e-4370" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix ${D},$ where \n\n$$\n{D}\n=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix ${D}$ has $1$ row and $4$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 4.$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088349 :question/math-academy-id "q-7810" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix $G$, where $G = \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$?" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $G$ has $3$ rows and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $3 \\times 1$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088350 :question/math-academy-id "q-7812" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix $J$ where $J = [3]$?" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $J$ has $1$ row and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $1 \\times 1$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259826 :question/math-academy-id "q-125543" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix $Y$, where $Y=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $Y$ has $1$ row  and $1$ column.  So,  its dimensions are  $1\\times 1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299742 :question/math-academy-id "q-319329" :question/problem "Let $S=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -\\sqrt{7} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nThe dimensions of the matrix $S$ are {{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $S$ has $4$ rows and $1$ column. Therefore, its dimensions are $4\\times 1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299743 :question/math-academy-id "q-319338" :question/problem "Let $E=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nThe dimensions of the matrix $E$ are {{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $E$ has $5$ rows and $1$ column. Therefore, its dimensions are $5\\times 1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348063 :question/math-academy-id "q-7809" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix $F$, where $F=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $F$ has $1$ row and $4$ columns.  Hence,  its dimensions are  $1\\times 4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348064 :question/math-academy-id "q-7813" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix $K$, where $K=\\begin{bmatrix}0\\end{bmatrix}?$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $K$ has $1$ row  and $1$ column.  So,  its dimensions are  $1\\times 1.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix with One Row or Column"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087511 :question/math-academy-id "e-5017" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix?\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1.1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nC = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $A$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2 \\times 3.$ $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has $3$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3 \\times 2.$ $\\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $1$ row and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1 \\times 3.$ $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix."} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088275 :question/math-academy-id "q-69514" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $1 \\times 2$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nC = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nD = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "B is 2 by 2, C is 2 by 1, and D is 1 by 2. Only D has the requested dimensions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088276 :question/math-academy-id "q-69515" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3 \\times 4$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nP = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nQ = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 12 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -0.1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nR = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "P is 2 by 2, Q is 4 by 3, and R is 3 by 4. Only R has the requested dimensions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233376 :question/math-academy-id "q-125550" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3\\times 2$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nE = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nF = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nG = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $E$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $F$ has $3$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 2.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $G$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, only $F$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259827 :question/math-academy-id "q-125552" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $2\\times 1$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nC = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nD = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $B$ has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 2.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $2$ rows and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 1.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $D$ has $1$ row and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 2.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, only $C$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335978 :question/math-academy-id "q-69518" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3\\times 2$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nL = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nM = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 5 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nN = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $L$ has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 2.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $M$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $N$ has $3$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 2.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $N$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335979 :question/math-academy-id "q-69523" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $4\\times 3$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nL = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nM = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 5 & \\sqrt{2} & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nN = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $L$ has $4$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $4\\times 3.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $M$ has $3$ rows and $4$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 4.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $N$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, only $L$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400184 :question/math-academy-id "q-125549" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $2\\times 2$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nP = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nQ = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nR = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $P$ has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 2.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $Q$ has $4$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $4\\times 2.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $R$ has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 2.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $P$ and $R$ are $2\\times 2$ matrices."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403328 :question/math-academy-id "q-69517" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3\\times 1$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nF = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nG = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nH = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $F$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $G$ has $3$ rows and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 1.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $H$ has $1$ row and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, only $G$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476006 :question/problem "Which of the following is a $1\\times 3$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nP = \\begin{bmatrix}-0.1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nQ = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nR = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -0.1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $P$ has $1$ row and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 1.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $Q$ has $1$ row and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 3.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $R$ has $3$ rows and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 1.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, only $Q$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476021 :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3\\times 3$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nP = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & -3 & -0.1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1.1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nQ = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 7 & -0.1 \\\\ -0.1 & 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nR = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & \\sqrt{3} & -0.1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $P$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $Q$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $R$ has $3$ rows and $4$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 4.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, only $P$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476046 :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3\\times 4$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1.1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{3} & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nC = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1.1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ -3 & \\sqrt{2} & 1.1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2} & -1 & 1.1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nD = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -3 & \\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -0.1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -0.1 & 7 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -0.1 & 0 & 1.1 & 1.1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $B$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $3$ rows and $4$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 4.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $D$ has $4$ rows and $4$ columns. So, its dimensions are $4\\times 4.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, only $C$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476058 :question/problem "Which of the following is a $4\\times 4$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nP = \\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 & 1.1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & -0.1 \\\\ 1 & 7 & -0.1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nQ = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & \\sqrt{3} & -0.1 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 1 & 1.1 & \\sqrt{2} & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nR = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 7 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2 & 5 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 1.1 & 5 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $P$ has $4$ rows and $4$ columns. So, its dimensions are $4\\times 4.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $Q$ has $3$ rows and $4$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 4.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $R$ has $4$ rows and $4$ columns. So, its dimensions are $4\\times 4.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $P$ and $R$ are $4\\times 4$ matrices."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476067 :question/problem "Which of the following is a $1\\times 3$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nP = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -0.1 & -0.1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nQ = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nR = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $P$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $Q$ has $3$ rows and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 1.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $R$ has $1$ row and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 3.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $R$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476075 :question/problem "Which of the following is a $2\\times 3$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -0.1 \\\\ 1.1 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nC = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1 \\\\ -0.1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nD = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -0.1 & -2 \\\\ 5 & \\sqrt{3} & -0.1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $B$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $3$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 2.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $D$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 3.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $D$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476085 :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3\\times 1$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nF = \\begin{bmatrix}1.1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nG = \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 1.1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nH = \\begin{bmatrix}4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $F$ has $3$ rows and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 1.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $G$ has $1$ row and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $H$ has $1$ row and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 1.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, only $F$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476094 :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3\\times 3$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nL = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0 \\\\ 7 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nM = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -3 & -0.1 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nN = \\begin{bmatrix}1.1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1.1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $L$ has $3$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 2.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $M$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $N$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $N$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476096 :question/problem "Which of the following is a $4\\times 3$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -0.1 \\\\ -0.1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -0.1 \\\\ -1 & 1.1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nC = \\begin{bmatrix}1.1 & \\sqrt{2} & -0.1 \\\\ -2 & -0.1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $A$ has $4$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $4\\times 2.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $B$ has $4$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $4\\times 3.$ $\\,✓$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3\\times 3.$ $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476097 :question/problem "Which of the following is a $1\\times 2$ matrix?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nE = \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nF = \\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nG = \\begin{bmatrix}1.1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $E$ has $1$ row and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 1.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $F$ has $2$ rows and $1$ column. So, its dimensions are $2\\times 1.$ $\\,\\times $\n\n- The matrix $G$ has $1$ row and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 2.$ $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, only $G$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix."}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087654 :question/math-academy-id "e-9942" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9af4801abdec165de2b720a21dd6a727c578e32cbae9c9c54bd589a1b65043b0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three train stations, where the nodes represent stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents the number of routes between the three stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}a}$</td><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$0$</td><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}b}$</td><td>$2$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}c}$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station A and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $A$ to $A,$ and so $a=1.$\n\n- No arrows are starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there are no routes from $B$ to $B,$ and so $b=0.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $C$ to $A,$ and so $c=1.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$0$</td><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td><td>$2$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+0+1 = 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088004 :question/math-academy-id "q-138940" :question/problem "![](images/cb/cbdf5c2470229ae3ba10ee18bccd2dc764407897397466b157474bf2b819f5d9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows bus routes between three stations, where nodes represent the stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents information about the number of routes between each of the three stations. Find the value of $a + b + c$.\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$0$</td><td>$a$</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$0$</td><td>$b$</td><td>$c$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>" :question/worked-solut