ding at Station B. So, there are three routes from $B$ to $B,$ and so $b=3.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $C$ to $A,$ and so $c=1.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$1$</td><td>$3$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$0$</td><td>$3$</td><td>$2$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$1$</td><td>$2$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c=3+3+1=7.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Matrices for Route Planning"}] :topic/title "Introduction to Matrices"}
